          "case_name": "Calabretta v. Floyd",
          "citation": "189 F.3d 808 (9th Cir. 1999)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit",
          "year": 1999,
          "date_decided": "1999-08-26",
          "holding": "The court affirmed denial of qualified immunity, holding that a social worker and police officer who coerced entry into a home to investigate an anonymous abuse report, interviewed a child and strip-searched another without a warrant or exigency violated clearly established Fourth and Fourteenth Amendment law.",
          "relevance": "The decision defines the constitutional limits on how an accepted investigation may be conducted in the home.",
          "quote": "In our circuit, a reasonable official would have known that the law barred this entry.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/7078750/calabretta-v-floyd/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 7078750
        },
